# Pre-audit deep gate — Remit v2, forge-std 1.16.2
#
# Command:  FOUNDRY_PROFILE=deep forge test
#           (env-var form, not --profile. This Foundry rejects that flag outright —
#            "error: unexpected argument '--profile' found" — and on a build that
#            accepted and ignored it the gate would quietly run the default profile,
#            which is the failure this form exists to prevent)
# Date:     2026-09-03, started 16:25:22Z, finished ~16:37:10Z (start plus real)
# Result:   276 passed, 0 failed, 0 skipped, exit 0
# Wall:     real 11m48.298s, user 56m30.161s, sys 0m1.314s (14 suites, ~4.8x parallel)
#           Foundry self-reports 2421.44s aggregate; it undercounts, see FORGE.md
#
# This supersedes deep.log as the current gate. deep.log stays where it is: that run is
# v1's gate, at 140 tests against forge-std 1.9.6, and it is the campaign the deployed
# bytecode at 0x3744E93B9e796E05CB66311d897559B6F3860196 was cleared by.
#
# Faster than v1 despite twice the tests — 708.298s against 951.857s wall, 3390.161s
# against 4887.476s user — and the cause is not established. The deep profile multiplies
# the same four testFuzz_ and three invariant_ functions in both runs, and the forge
# config key set is identical across the two dumps at 137 keys with none added and none
# removed, so a config-visible Foundry change is ruled out and a patch-level one is not.
# Machine load and the v2 invariant handler's call mix are the other candidates. Recorded
# rather than explained, because an unexplained speedup in a gate is worth someone's
# attention: a gate that got faster by doing less work would look exactly like this.
#
# The forge config dump below is the CONTROL: it is what proves the deep profile was
# actually resolved. Look for fuzz runs = 20000, invariant runs = 2000, depth = 256,
# shrink_run_limit = 20000, and optimizer_runs = 200 matching the default profile. A
# gate that compiles different bytecode is not a gate.
#
### CONFIG PROOF ###
[profile.deep]
